To understand the recursive definition of the sequence $ a(n) $, we begin by analyzing the given sequence values:

$$
\begin{align*}
a(0) &= 1, \\
a(1) &= 2, \\
a(2) &= 2, \\
a(3) &= 3, \\
a(4) &= 3, \\
a(5) &= 4, \\
a(6) &= 4, \\
a(7) &= 4, \\
a(8) &= 5, \\
a(9) &= 5, \\
a(10) &= 5, \\
a(11) &= 6, \\
a(12) &= 6, \\
a(13) &= 6, \\
a(14) &= 6, \\
a(15) &= 7, \\
a(16) &= 7, \\
a(17) &= 7, \\
a(18) &= 7, \\
a(19) &= 8, \\
a(20) &= 8, \\
a(21) &= 8, \\
a(22) &= 8, \\
a(23) &= 9, \\
a(24) &= 9.
\end{align*}
$$

---

### Observing the Pattern

We notice that the values of $ a(n) $ increase by 1 at specific positions:  
$$
n = 1, 3, 5, 8, 11, 15, 19, 23, \dots
$$  
At these indices, $ a(n) $ increases by 1 compared to $ a(n-1) $. Elsewhere, $ a(n) = a(n-1) $.

Let’s define these positions as **threshold indices**, denoted $ t_k $, where the value $ a(n) $ increments.

---

### Recursive Definition

We can now define the sequence $ a(n) $ recursively as follows:

$$
\begin{aligned}
a(0) &= 1 \\
a(n) &= 
\begin{cases}
a(n-1) + 1, & \text{if } n \text{ is a threshold index} \\
a(n-1), & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\end{aligned}
$$

Here, the **threshold indices** $ t_k $ are defined by the recurrence:

$$
\begin{aligned}
t_1 &= 1 \\
t_k &= t_{k-1} + \left\lfloor \frac{k+1}{2} \right\rfloor
\end{aligned}
$$

This recurrence generates the threshold indices:

$$
t_k = 1, 3, 5, 8, 11, 15, 19, 23, 28, \dots
$$

This matches the observed positions where $ a(n) $ increases by 1.

---

### Final Recursive Formula

Putting it all together, the **recursive formula** for the sequence $ a(n) $ is:

$$
\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
a(0) &= 1 \\
a(n) &= 
\begin{cases}
a(n-1) + 1, & \text{if } n = t_k \text{ for some } k \\
a(n-1), & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\end{aligned}
}
$$

Where the **threshold indices** $ t_k $ are defined by:

$$
\boxed{
t_1 = 1, \quad t_k = t_{k-1} + \left\lfloor \frac{k+1}{2} \right\rfloor \text{ for } k \geq 2
}
$$

---

### Summary

- The sequence $ a(n) $ increases by 1 at specific **threshold indices**.
- These indices are defined by a recurrence involving the floor function.
- The recursive definition of $ a(n) $ depends on whether $ n $ is one of these threshold indices.)

